From: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Subject: [PATCH 33/33] cuda/hip: extend the GDN conv-chain fold to the prefill shape, deleting concat_non_cont (+3.6% prefill)

Round-13's prefill profile put `concat_non_cont` at 4.7% of pp512 (30 x 188 us),
and a fresh profile of the r13+0032 build reproduces it (30 x 165 us per pass,
4.1% of 240 ms). It is pure layout churn. Every GDN layer builds its conv input
as

    conv_input = CONCAT(state_window[3, C], TRANSPOSE(qkv_mixed)[n_t, C])

- a [3+n_t, C] TIME-major buffer assembled by a scalar-indexed kernel from a
CHANNEL-major activation - only for `ggml_ssm_conv` to read it back out
channel by channel and write a channel-major result. At pp512 that is 16.8 MB
read non-contiguously plus 16.8 MB written, per layer, at ~170 GB/s, five
times the cost of the convolution it feeds.

0031 already collapses CONCAT + SSM_CONV + SILU + the conv-state writeback CPY
into one kernel, but only at n_t == 1. This patch generalises that fold to
n_t > 1 (and to n_s > 1, see below): one thread owns a channel, walks
`qkv_mixed` in its natural channel-major layout with the d_conv-wide window in
registers, and writes the silu of the convolution straight to its destination.
The concat never happens; per pass 30 concat_non_cont (165 us), 30
ssm_conv_long_token_f32 (39 us) and 30 writeback cpys become 30 fold kernels
(38 us) plus a staging kernel below the noise floor. Kernel time per pp512 pass
drops 240.3 -> 224.3 ms and the fold kernel runs at an effective 876 GB/s
(the activation is still MALL-resident from the projection that produced it).

Two hazards, both real and both load-bearing:

1. ALIASING. `ggml-alloc` places the conv/silu output exactly on top of
   `qkv_mixed` - the CONCAT is that activation's last consumer, so it is dead
   the moment the fold starts - which means the kernel writes its input.
   Per channel that is safe: output token i is emitted only after column
   i+d_conv-1 is already in registers, and every later output of that channel
   reads it from the register window, never from memory. It is NOT safe across
   token chunks (a chunk's d_conv-1 prologue columns are the previous chunk's
   outputs) nor for the conv-state writeback (which reads the last d_conv-1
   input columns). So the chunked path stages both, from the still-pristine
   input, in a separate kernel before any output is written; and the detector
   declines outright unless the two tensors are EXACTLY co-located or fully
   disjoint, since any other overlap would cross channels.
   When one chunk covers the whole ubatch - every server prompt shorter than
   GGML_SSM_CONV_FOLD_SPLIT (default 64) - a second kernel takes over that
   needs no staging at all: the window it must write back is still in the
   thread's registers after the last output. No scratch, no pool allocation,
   one launch.

2. THE GATE CORPUS RUNS n_s = 4. `llama-perplexity` packs n_batch/n_ctx
   sequences into one ubatch, so the gate corpus at `-c 512` evaluates
   n_t = 128, n_s = 4 - and the 0031 detector required n_s == 1. The first
   version of this patch was therefore never exercised by the perplexity gate
   at all while looking perfectly healthy. The detector and both kernels now
   carry the sequence dimension. Anything that measures this fold must first
   confirm it FIRES there: GGML_CUDA_SSM_CONV_FOLD_DEBUG=1 prints one
   accept/decline line per layer, with the numbered gate that declined.

Signed zero: the stock kernels accumulate from a literal 0.0f that their
codegen keeps, while the fold's is folded away, so an all-zero window landed on
-0.0 where stock produces +0.0 - value-equal, but the sign survives silu into
the rest of the graph. An arithmetic `+ 0.0f` is legal to optimise away and
duly was; the fold normalises the bit pattern instead, at one VALU op per
output.

GGML_CUDA_DISABLE_SSM_CONV_FOLD_SEQ=1 restores the stock prefill chain (the
n_t == 1 decode fold from 0031 is untouched by that toggle);
GGML_SSM_CONV_FOLD_SPLIT and GGML_SSM_CONV_FOLD_BLOCK tune the chunked path.
GGML_SSM_CONV_DUMP / GGML_SSM_WB_DUMP / GGML_SSM_STATE_DUMP write the conv
output, the writeback triple and the decode-entry state to disk in a layout
that is directly comparable between the two arms; they are how the numbers
below were obtained and they skip themselves while a graph is capturing.

Measured (runner box, HIP_VISIBLE_DEVICES=0):

- same-binary toggle A/B, 5 interleaved rounds: pp512 4155.2-4195.1 off ->
  4302.3-4359.2 on, median per-round ratio 1.0355 (arms fully disjoint,
  5/5); tg128 neutral (137.3-137.7 both arms)
- whole-process stock vs cand, 5 rounds: see README
- split sweep (pp512): 16 -> 4285, 32 -> 4294, 64 -> 4268, 128 -> 4288,
  256 -> 4250, 512 (single chunk) -> 4176. Flat over 16-128; 64 shipped.
- gate ppl: 3.9288 / 3.9261 / 3.9271 / 3.9271 / 3.9267 vs stock 3.9314
  (-0.07% to -0.13%, band 0.5%); the fold-off control in the same session
  reads 3.9262-3.9390
- test-backend-ops SSM_CONV / CONCAT / CPY / UNARY / GATED_DELTA_NET /
  MUL_MAT all OK
- numerics, measured directly rather than argued: over a full 512-token
  prefill (n_t=512, n_s=1) all 30 GDN conv outputs are BYTE-IDENTICAL to
  stock, as is the conv-state writeback; likewise at n_t = 2, 4, 5, 8, 11,
  12, 16, 64, 128 and at n_s = 2 and 4. From the SECOND ubatch of a sequence
  - i.e. once the conv state is no longer zero - the 4-term dot product
  contracts differently than the stock kernel's and ~10% of outputs move by
  1 ULP (2 ULP was never observed). That is the same ppl-gated class as
  0024/0026/0028/0031 and the mildest instance of it in the series.
- server greedy: the candidate is deterministic (6/6 byte-exact A/A); vs the
  fold-off control 2/6 byte-exact and 4/6 drift at 9-58% prefixes, which is
  the 1-ULP class above, not a state error - the writeback was verified
  directly against the stock arm's materialised conv_input.

---

